[38;5;239m[48;5;237m❯ [38;5;231mThe second: no backend yet, but there will be one. We're free to define what login returns.[39m
[49m
  [38;5;246mRead [1m2[0m[38;5;246m files [39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m (no modules, no bundler), and [38;5;153msrc/[39m is unrelated CommonJS Node
  code with no test runner configured. So a shared identity layer has to pick a loading story — I'll bring that as part
  of the design, not as a question now.

  Next question, because it decides how much we're building:

  [1mWhat does the stored identity actually drive?[0m "Track who logged in" can mean two very different systems, and the gap
  between them is a security boundary, not a feature.

  - [1mAttribution only[0m — the ID labels analytics events, console/audit logs, and maybe a "Signed in as…" display. Nothing
    trusts it for access. We store one small thing and read it from anywhere. Small, and it stays small.
  - [1mIt also gates behavior[0m — some form, route, or UI element is shown/hidden or allowed/denied based on who's logged in.
    The moment that's true, a client-stored ID is not sufficient: anyone can edit localStorage and become any user.
    That design needs a server-issued token alongside the ID, and every gated action has to be enforced server-side,
    with the client state as a hint only.

  The distinction matters now rather than later because it changes what the login response contract should contain — and
  you said we're free to define that. Getting the token into the contract from the start is nearly free; retrofitting
  it after other forms are already reading a trusted-looking ID is not.

  Which is it — and if it's the second, is enforcement expected to be part of this work or just the client-side plumbing
  for it?

